<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>
  <!--
    The solution-wide Directory.Build.props is imported by RELATIVE path, not by
    the GetPathOfFileAbove function. MSBuild evaluates both to the same file
    (this directory's parent), but the project-file analyzer (#1857) can only
    follow an import it can resolve on disk, and a property function is not
    resolvable to it: with the function form the analyzer never sees the
    solution-wide props, so the properties pinned there (NuGetAuditMode,
    NuGetAuditLevel) look unset and the rules that read them fire.
  -->
  <Import Project="../Directory.Build.props"/>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CA1707;CA1861;S5445;S3011</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    TUNIT'S OWN ANALYZERS ARE A GATE HERE, NOT A COMMENT (#1836).

    TUnit 1.75 ships two analyzer assemblies inside its own NuGet packages —
    TUnit.Analyzers (in TUnit.Core) and TUnit.Assertions.Analyzers (in
    TUnit.Assertions) — and NuGet hands both to the compiler as analyzers
    automatically. They were already RUNNING on this tree before #1836; what
    was missing is the part that makes a finding mean something: the
    diagnostics below are promoted to ERRORS for every test project, so a new
    violation fails the build instead of scrolling past as a warning.

    HOW THE PROMOTION WORKS, AND WHY IT IS NOT `TreatWarningsAsErrors`
    The property above sets TreatWarningsAsErrors=false on purpose: the
    test tree carries documented false positives (RS0030, CFE0001) and
    "treat everything as an error" would train people to ignore the analyzer
    rather than fix it. `WarningsAsErrors` is the OTHER half of the same
    switch and is INDEPENDENT of it: the compiler gets /warnaserror+:<ids>
    next to /warnaserror-, and a specific warning id always wins over the
    general setting. So the tree keeps its lenient default and these two
    rules are strict.

    THE TWO RULES
    TUnitAssertions0005 — "Assert.That(...) should not be used with a constant
      value". The rule fires when the FIRST argument of Assert.That is a
      compile-time constant. Two shapes exist in this tree, and they get
      opposite verdicts:
        * a literal (`Assert.That(true).IsTrue()`) — a REAL DEFECT. The
          assertion cannot fail, so the test proves nothing. Fixed: the
          tautology is replaced by the assertion the test actually means.
        * a production constant compared against a literal
          (`Assert.That(ContextUsage.WarnThreshold).IsEqualTo(0.50)`) — the
          constant IS the subject. A change to the constant recompiles the
          assertion to a different value and the test fails, so it is a
          change detector, not a tautology. Waived at the call site with a
          written reason (the repo convention, same as RS0030).

    TUnitAssertions0015 — "Use .IsTrue() instead of .IsEqualTo(true)". The
      same defect written in a worse spelling; the fix is mechanical and
      needs no waiver.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT PROMOTED
    TUnit0055 (Console.SetOut inside a test — a real race, owned by #1790's
    ParallelLimiter work), TUnit0015 (a CancellationToken parameter with no
    [Timeout] — the CTS boilerplate ticket) and TUnit0023 (a disposable not
    released in a cleanup method) stay WARNINGS here. Promoting them would
    mean either a blanket NoWarn per project (which is the thing this
    repository forbids) or a behaviour change at the 42 sites those three
    rules name (12 + 29 + 1, measured on the build log of run 39421864352),
    which belongs to its own ticket. They are still reported on every build,
    so the count is visible; only the enforcement is deferred, and the reason
    is written here so the deferral is a decision rather than an oversight.

    THE CONTROL: tools/check-tunit-analyzer-gate.py, run by ci.yml, builds
    tests/TUnitAnalyzerCanary — a project that is NOT in Harbor.slnx and is
    therefore never part of the normal build — and asserts that the
    planted offender in it is reported as an error while the planted WAIVED
    offender next to it is not. If the analyzer stopped shipping, or this
    promotion stopped applying, or a waiver grew past its own site, that
    check goes red.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);TUnitAssertions0005;TUnitAssertions0015</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
